evolution-jmap (0.6.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Added a Server Filters page to the account editor for servers that
    support JMAP Sieve filters. It lists and manages filter scripts, and
    offers a rules list for one managed script so most users never need
    to write Sieve by hand. If another program changes the filters while
    the page is open, the page does not overwrite that change.
  * This release replaces 0.6.0, whose release build failed before
    publishing. See the v0.6.1 tag message for detail.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:53:46 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Calendars and address books that another user shares are now shown as
    additional collections, so they can be browsed and, with the right
    permissions, edited. Deleting a shared calendar or address book now
    acts on the correct account and is only offered when the user
    actually has permission to remove it.
  * An address book or calendar without write access now opens read-only,
    so Evolution greys out editing instead of letting a save fail
    afterwards.
  * A shared calendar or address book's display name can no longer be set
    by its owner to hide who shared it: the name is now always "owner:
    collection name".
  * The server now sends meeting invitations, updates and cancellations
    itself. A clear error is shown when the server cannot do this for one
    of the guests, and Evolution's own "do not send invitation" option
    still suppresses a single change. A plain import or backup restore of
    a meeting no longer mails its organizer by mistake, and neither does
    removing an invitation that was already cancelled.
  * Dismissing a calendar reminder is now sent to the server, so the same
    reminder does not fire again on your other devices.
  * Editing a contact or event that was changed elsewhere at the same
    time now follows Evolution's own conflict choice (keep mine, keep the
    server's, or fail) instead of silently overwriting the other change.
  * An "Undo" notice now appears after deleting a calendar or address
    book collection.
  * Contact photos are now uploaded and downloaded through the server's
    blob storage, handling larger images without encoding them inline.
    You are told when a photo is too large for the server to store.
  * Added a "Load image" option for an externally hosted (http or https)
    contact photo in the address book preview: a placeholder and the URL
    are shown instead of loading the image automatically, and the photo
    is fetched only on request.
  * Snooze is now reachable by right-clicking a message's body, not only
    through its menu button, including in a detached message window.
    Fixed the "Next Monday Morning" snooze preset landing on the wrong
    day.
  * Fixed newly created mail folders becoming invisible on servers that
    default new folders to unsubscribed.
  * Fixed a deadlock that could freeze Evolution while an address book
    refreshed in the background.
  * Fixed a pushed folder change (for example, creating the Snoozed
    folder) sometimes jumping the mail view to a different account.
  * Deleting a message now moves it to Trash as usual; it is only
    permanently removed when Trash is emptied.
  * Fixed Send Later sometimes choosing the wrong outgoing identity.
  * Large batches of changes that exceed a server's per-request limit are
    now split automatically instead of failing outright.
  * Server rate-limit responses are now retried automatically with a
    backoff, without duplicating any writes.
  * The JMAP client now refuses a server redirect that would drop the
    connection from https to http, so credentials are never sent over a
    plain connection.
  * Updated the bundled TLS library (rustls-webpki, webpki-roots) to
    close two published security advisories (RUSTSEC-2026-0381 and
    RUSTSEC-2026-0382).
  * Updated the German translation (fully translated, no missing or
    fuzzy strings).

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 07:49:38 +0000

evolution-jmap (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a brand-new account silently signing in twice on its first
    connect, caused by its own setup accidentally triggering a second
    sign-in.
  * Editing a JMAP account's server address now triggers a fresh sign-in
    instead of needing Evolution restarted to take effect.
  * Fixed an endless chain of sign-in prompts when an account's server
    address ends in a trailing dot (for example api.fastmail.com.): the
    dot names the same server and is now treated that way, credentials
    survive the server's redirect, and a redirect that cannot carry them
    is reported as a clear connection error instead of another consent
    window.
  * A redirect to a malformed address from the server is now reported as
    an error instead of crashing the connection attempt.
  * A new account's Sent and Drafts folders are now configured
    automatically from the server's own folder roles, the same way
    Inbox, Trash and Junk already were.
  * Searching a folder's message bodies is now answered by the server, on
    evolution-data-server 3.58 and newer, instead of silently finding
    nothing.
  * An account whose saved sync state the server no longer recognises
    (for example after the server was restored from backup) now
    resynchronises automatically instead of getting stuck until its local
    cache is deleted by hand.
  * On evolution-data-server 3.62 and newer, the mail cache is written
    atomically, protecting it against corruption if Evolution is
    interrupted mid-write.
  * On evolution-data-server 3.63.1 and newer, OAuth 2.0 account setup
    uses evolution-data-server's own OAuth2Dynamic support instead of
    this project's own implementation. There is no migration: an
    existing account using the old method needs to be set up again after
    upgrading to such a build.
  * A round of interoperability fixes against real JMAP servers (tested
    against Stalwart): folder creation, calendar participants and
    notifications, and shared-resource ownership now tolerate several
    real-server quirks that used to produce spurious errors.
  * Setting up or reconnecting several JMAP accounts at once no longer
    lets one slow or unreachable server's address lookup delay the
    others.
  * Added a German translation.
  * Updated the bundled TLS library (rustls) to close four published
    security advisories (RUSTSEC-2026-0346 through RUSTSEC-2026-0349).
  * Mail, contacts and calendars have all been verified against a real
    JMAP server at real-world scale (up to 10,000 messages, 5,000
    contacts and 3,000 calendar events, including through
    evolution-data-server itself), with no correctness problems found.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:52:07 -0700

evolution-jmap (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rebuild of 0.4.0 through the repaired release pipeline; no code changes.
    The container image the pipeline builds the .deb in was orphaned when
    the repository was re-created, so 0.4.0's release workflow could not
    run and its .deb was uploaded by hand, without the pipeline's Sigstore
    provenance. The image is now published as this repository's own
    ci-image package and the workflow pins its digest; 0.4.1's artifacts
    are pipeline-built and attested again.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 12:12:48 -0700

evolution-jmap (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * JMAP push: the mail, calendar and address-book backends subscribe to the
    server's EventSource channel and refresh when the server announces a
    change, instead of waiting for the next poll. The folder list picks up
    mailboxes created or renamed elsewhere, the subscription follows OAuth2
    token refreshes, and repeated identical announcements are deduplicated.
  * A delivery arriving over push no longer steals the folder selection: a
    message landing in a mailbox you were not looking at used to jump the
    folder tree away from the folder you had selected. The folder list is
    now marked stale only when the tree's shape actually changed.
  * Out of Office: a vacation autoresponder page in the account editor, so
    the server's autoresponder can be switched on, dated and worded without
    leaving Evolution.
  * Send Later: the composer can hand a message to the server for delivery
    at a chosen time, on servers that offer delayed submission.
  * Snooze: the message-list context menu can put a message aside until a
    later time, on servers that offer it.
  * New Address Book / New Calendar: JMAP now appears in the Type list, and
    editing an existing JMAP book or calendar no longer crashes the dialog.
  * Folder searches the server can evaluate are answered by Email/query on
    the server instead of only the locally cached summary.
  * Folder properties now report the server's quota (RFC 9425), and a server
    that offers no quota at all is no longer reported as a server error.
  * Each account keeps its own OAuth 2.0 token slot, so two accounts with
    the same user name on different servers no longer share one token. This
    needs evolution-data-server 3.62 or newer to take effect; on 3.52 it is
    recorded and ignored.
  * Deleting a collection the server forbids deleting is greyed out instead
    of failing after the click.
  * A newly created address book or calendar keeps the name you typed; it
    could previously show the server's id as its name.
  * No sign-in window is offered when there is no keyring to keep the token;
    a PKCE consent that could never be redeemed is refused up front.
  * Contact photos whose bytes look like base64 are no longer decoded twice,
    and saving an unchanged contact or event no longer writes a spurious
    patch to the server.
  * Rebasing server-reported URLs onto the session origin is now a
    per-account setting, so a tunnelled self-hosted account and a public
    account can coexist in one process.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 09:07:46 -0700

evolution-jmap (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the hourly OAuth2 re-consent: a long-lived backend connection kept
    an expired access token and escalated its 401 to a consent window instead
    of refreshing. All four connection paths (calendar, address book, mail
    store, mail transport) now refresh once and retry. Operator-validated
    against a real account: seven token expiries handled silently, no consent
    windows.
  * Fixed mail body downloads against real servers: a cross-origin redirect on
    the blob download is now refused rather than returned as if it were the
    message.
  * Fixed the Inbox showing "no messages" until the user switched folders and
    back.
  * A dead Evolution instance's stale OAuth2 D-Bus proxy is now reported as
    itself rather than misclassified as a keyring failure.
  * Account setup and OAuth 2.0 improvements, especially against Fastmail.
  * Calendar and address-book creation and deletion.
  * A large batch of contact and calendar field-mapping fidelity work, driven
    by real-exporter fixtures, property-based tests and mutation testing.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:36:58 -0700

